WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.509
Speaker 1: We are really now for the first
time seeing the rapid decline of coronavirus

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:06.509
cases.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:09.870
In some states, it's down to almost a statistical
zero.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:15.840
It's not literally zero cases, but it's statistically
low enough where there's really no spread.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.699
Most people who are getting sick are not passing
it to anyone, and that's how you reduce the

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:22.099
number of cases.

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:24.160
This is just a good thing.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.419
Let's look at the latest numbers, starting
with U.S. cases nationally, which have declined

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:34.600
now more than 93 percent from their peak.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.530
The peak was 250, 5000 cases per day in late
January, now down early January, now down

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:44.039
below 18000 cases per day.

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:45.660
We are really getting there.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.929
The deaths are fortunately starting to come
down with them.

00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:56.140
Deaths are now down about 87 percent from
their peak below 500 a day from the year for

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.020
the first time since March of twenty.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.370
Twenty deaths are at their lowest in about
15 months.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.479
Now, is there anywhere in the US where cases
are going up?

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:08.479
Not really.

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:13.040
There are places where they sort of aren't
going down anymore and they tend to be places

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.570
with low rates of vaccination.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:18.880
So Mississippi is an example where cases have
been level for about ten weeks.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.970
They're not growing, but they're not really
shrinking anymore.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:23.329
That's not great.

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:24.440
They need to vaccinate.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.259
But lots of people there don't want the vaccine.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:32.170
It's likely that eventually the places with
lower vaccination rates are going to just

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:37.490
see continued clusters while the highly vaccinated
states are going to go down to, again, statistically

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:44.009
zero, not literally zero, but almost in terms
of globally in South America, things are not

00:01:44.009 --> 00:01:45.009
good.

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:48.460
And I was just looking at what's going on
in my birth country of Argentina.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.410
And it's a disaster.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.299
But overall, it's not just American cases
and deaths that are coming down.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:59.920
Global cases are now declining pretty rapidly
from their peak at one point globally at eight

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.020
hundred and thirty thousand new global cases
per day.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:10.190
That's now down about 40 percent to four hundred
eighty five thousand cases per day, down almost

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.390
half.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:18.890
And then global deaths are also more slowly
declining, but down about twenty five percent

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:19.890
from their peak.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:25.989
At one point 14000 humans per day were dying
from covid.

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:30.920
We still have about 11000 dying per day of
this virus.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:36.390
Overwhelmingly, it's happening in the least
vaccinated countries.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:41.920
Re restating, underlining the importance of
getting vaccines to all parts of the world.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.709
This is not over, but in some places it will
effectively be over soon.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:50.620
More mask mandates are being pulled in.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.900
States were pulled over Memorial Day weekend.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:59.519
The vaccines work well against the variants,
so it's not time to go crazy and do stupid

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:00.519
stuff.

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:03.129
I think at this point we all know what is
what is considered stupid at this point in

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:04.129
time.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:08.590
Large gatherings of unvaccinated people indoors
is basically the stupidest thing you can do.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:14.340
But vaccinated people can do most things with
what we would call baseline risk.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.750
And this is a really important concept to
understand, because psychologically the pandemic

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:25.549
has really shaken up our concept of the risks
we accept by existing in the world.

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:28.470
Risk wasn't zero before this pandemic.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.560
You could go out and you could get a cold,
you could go out and you could get the flu.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.440
And sometimes people die of the flu.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.280
You could go out and pick up a stomach bug
through physical contact or in a restaurant

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.159
or whatever you could get in a car accident.

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:47.409
So the question for me is not when is the
risk of covid zero, but it's when is it low

00:03:47.409 --> 00:03:53.689
enough that we would consider when I go out,
I'm at baseline risk, meaning I'm accepting

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:59.340
that generally there's some risk to being
in the world, but there's not this acute thing.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.319
And vaccines are a huge part of getting back
to that baseline level of risk, of just being

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:07.069
alive and existing in the world, even staying
home.

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:08.300
You could fall down the stairs.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.300
I get it.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.300
Even staying staying home.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.500
You could poison yourself with lead paint.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.500
I don't know.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:20.540
But but the point here is there are lots of
people who before this pandemic were accepting

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.450
risks by being out in the world that maybe
they didn't even quantify.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.270
And I think that now, because we're so aware
of it, it's going to feel scarier.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:38.080
And like I get it, I was, you know, the other
day the grocery store there just before more

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.610
mass guidelines were pulled, the.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.700
They were already a bunch of people without
masks, I was wearing my mask because that

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.230
was the rules at the time and I see the people
without the mask and it's like, whoa, whoa.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.360
It's like you visualize a stream of germs
coming out of their mouth.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.940
We accepted that before, right?

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.830
For my 30 whatever years that I lived until
the pandemic.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.230
I accepted that and didn't think twice about
it.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.580
The question is, when will we be back to that?

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.170
And then is that an acceptable level of risk
for some people?

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:07.860
Going back to the old level of risk, for example,
of flu won't be acceptable and people are

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.040
going to continue wearing a mask, and that's
fine, but it's going back to normal.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.390
But it's sort of a new normal where I think
all of us are more aware of the risk to just

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:18.780
being out in the world now.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:19.940
One last thing.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.630
Vaccination keeps slowing down.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:27.440
In the US, even though there are tons more
people to vaccinate, daily vaccine doses are

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.540
down to one point five million a day.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.220
At one point, they were more than double that
three point three million a day.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.880
There are tons of people still to vaccinate.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.380
If they don't get vaccinated, some are going
to get covered and some are going to die.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:46.570
And many doctors believe that because this
is going to keep circulating if you're not

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:50.880
vaccinated, that it's almost certain at some
point you will get it something to think about.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:51.880
And that.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.160
Let's now talk a little more about the psychological
impact of this pandemic

